==Old English==

===Alternative spellings===
* [[sex#Old English|sex]]
* [[syx#Old English|syx]]

===Etymology===
From {{proto|Germanic|seks|lang=ang}}, whence also Old Frisian {{term|sex|lang=ofs}}, Old Saxon {{term|sehs|lang=osx}}, Middle Dutch {{term|sesse}} (Dutch {{term|zes|lang=nl}}), Old High German {{term|sehs|lang=goh}} (German {{term|sechs}}), Old Norse [[sex]] (Swedish [[sex]]), Gothic {{term|𐍃𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍃|tr=saihs|sc=Goth}}. Ultimately from {{proto|Indo-European|swéḱs|lang=ang}}, cognate with Greek {{term|ἕξ|tr=héx|sc=polytonic}}, Latin {{term|sex|lang=la}}, Old Irish {{term|se|sē|lang=sga}} (Irish {{term|sé|lang=ga}}), Old Slavic ''šestʼ'' (Russian {{term|шесть|tr=šestʼ}}).

